{
  "hypothesis": {
    "id": "post-fault-client-ops-probabilistic-dose",
    "parent": "post-fault-client-ops-sweep-retry",
    "kind": "add",
    "title": "Sub-integer dosing: apply post-fault client ops with probability p per fault",
    "description": "The measured optimum sits at the smallest nonzero integer dose (1), with 0 and 2 both worse on d5-d8, so the true optimum is unresolvable on the integer grid. Add a scalar probability field (e.g. post_fault_client_ops_prob, default 1.0) that gates whether the post-fault client-op burst fires at all for a given fault, keeping the burst size at 1. Sweep p in {0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0} on VR.spur as measurement-only exposure (expect campaign-aggregate reject; read the per-arm curve), then promote the argmax as a single-arm candidate. Report depthAtLeast[4..8] and rewardRate per arm; the dose-3 rewardRate rise (186 vs 151/s) suggests too much client work terminates runs early, so p<1 may recover deep prefixes without paying that cost.",
    "category": "config",
    "buildsOn": [
      "config_override",
      "post_fault_client_ops"
    ],
    "expectedGain": 3,
    "expectedCost": 1.5,
    "rationale": "The inverted-U peaks at the grid's minimum nonzero point, which is exactly the signature of an optimum hiding between 0 and 1; probabilistic dosing is the only way to interpolate a discrete count knob.",
    "generalityArgument": "Probabilistic gating to interpolate an integer scheduler knob is a generic technique, independent of protocol or spec.",
    "status": "proposed",
    "branch": null,
    "prUrls": [],
    "createdAtIso": "2026-08-29T10:15:00.000Z",
    "firingCounter": null,
    "notes": "[unparked iteration 5363] Peak-at-boundary integer dose (0 and 2 both worse than 1) is genuinely unresolved and the dose rides as an overlay in the existing grid-post-fault-2 arm, replacing an integer tunable rather than adding one; falsifier: no p beats integer 1 on d>=6/s."
  },
